# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 have "removed → mixed" and "mixed ∧ owns_labour → property"; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 has "removed → mixed" and "mixed → property"; they differ when only "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" and "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" are true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" and "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" are true and "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" and "the thing is made his property" are false. Is S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then?
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r2 has "removed ∧ enough_left → excludes"; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 have "removed → property"; they differ when only "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "the thing has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but the labourer can have a right to it" are true, which the first allows
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: locke-labour.logic.r2 puts it at S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others."; locke-labour.logic.r2.2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3 at S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property."
locke-labour.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r2.2 and locke-labour.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others.", rather than S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property."?
